(Page 23 of 49 - 04 - Agent Tools & Interoperability) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability) Tj T*
(May 2026) Tj T*
(23) Tj T*
("Does the caller need a result, or does the caller need another participant to take) Tj T*
(responsibility?" -- that's the cleanest framing I've seen for this decision. The smell test) Tj T*
(of a central agent prompt growing into an accidental workflow engine is exactly how) Tj T*
(most teams discover they needed collaboration semantics three months too late.19) Tj T*
(But this raises the next architectural question: If we are just delegating to external specialists,) Tj T*
(why can't we just treat them as standard tools?) Tj T*
(The nature of the engagement with specialists and tools is fundamentally different. Imagine) Tj T*
(a homeowner renovating a kitchen. They face a choice: purchase the individual tools and) Tj T*
(manuals to attempt the build themselves, or delegate the project to a domain specialist who) Tj T*
(builds kitchens for a living.) Tj T*
(Tools are just passive instruments; a specialist is a collaborative partner. When you hire that) Tj T*
(specialist, you don't just hand them a single blueprint and walk away expecting a perfect) Tj T*
(kitchen to magically appear. They will hit edge cases or an oversight in your original design) Tj T*
(requiring them to pause, consult you on trade-offs, and resume.) Tj T*
(Bounded vs. Unbounded Domains) Tj T*
(This is exactly one of the reasons why treating a specialist AI agent as a simple tool does not) Tj T*
(scale. A standard tool or API operates on a fire-and-forget mechanism. It expects a single,) Tj T*
(perfectly formatted request \(the blueprint payload\) and returns a response. Real-world) Tj T*
(software environments often contain the digital equivalent of crooked walls: ambiguous data) Tj T*
(structures, misleading requirements, and conflicting user preferences.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
