(Here's what an A2UI message looks like \(v0.9 format\):) Tj T*
(Snippet 4: Example A2UI message) Tj T*
(Components form a flat adjacency list referenced by id, which makes the structure easy for) Tj T*
(an LLM to generate incrementally and easy for the client to update without re-rendering. A) Tj T*
(separate createSurface message \(not shown\) tells the client which id is the root. The client) Tj T*
(then renders this as a complete, interactive interface: no React code required.) Tj T*
(Generating A2UI: Two Patterns) Tj T*
(There are two ways to produce A2UI in practice, and the choice is mostly about where the) Tj T*
(layout decision lives.) Tj T*
(JSON) Tj T*
({) Tj T*
("version": "v0.9",) Tj T*
("updateComponents": {) Tj T*
("surfaceId": "main",) Tj T*
("components": [) Tj T*
({"id": "root", "component": "Column", "children": ["title",) Tj T*
("summary", "export"]},) Tj T*
({"id": "title", "component": "Text", "text": "Q4 Sales", "variant": "h1"},) Tj T*
({"id": "summary", "component": "Text", "text": "Revenue grew 12% QoQ"},) Tj T*
({"id": "export", "component": "Button", "child": "export-label",) Tj T*
("action": {"event": {"name": "export_csv"}}},) Tj T*
({"id": "export-label", "component": "Text", "text": "Export CSV"}) Tj T*
(]) Tj T*
(}) Tj T*
(}) Tj T*
